<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="77c1e3a1">&lt;p&gt;
    Milestones are significant points or events in the project. The main objective of establishing milestones is to provide
    a measurement of the project’s progress and success, and to allow early re-assessment of the ability to meet critical
    timelines. Per PMBOK, a milestone is a significant point or event in the project. The EY PM methodology expands on this
    definition by stating that a milestone marks a point in time for which at least one of the below is true:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Marks the completion of a group of related activities or deliverables
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is where key decisions or key deliverables must be completed for the project to advance
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is on the critical path for the project’s completion
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Can be tied to another project such that achievement of one of the project’s milestones may impact progress on
        other projects
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is agreed as a key point in the project by the project team, key stakeholders and sponsors
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Milestones are most effective when all parties with a stake in the project are involved in their selection. Milestones
    are internal or external to the project, and create high-level visibility and accountability. Thus, it is important to
    examine non-project factors that may impact a particular milestone. Capturing an external event(s) as a milestone will
    help others understand events outside of the PMgr’s control.
&lt;/p&gt;
